## Winning design
Single fused Triton kernel implementing the whole AltUp predict→correct backward:
- **Design B row-blocking**: grid = `cdiv(rows, BLOCK_ROWS)`, `BLOCK_ROWS=16`,
  each program owns 16 consecutive rows.
- **Single-pass, fully H-resident** per row: `BLOCK_H=next_pow2(2304)=4096`
  masked; every heavy tensor (`hidden[3]`, `grad_corrected[3]`, `activated`,
  `norm_weight`, `router_weight[3]`) read exactly once.
- **Shared-weight amortization**: `router_weight`/`norm_weight` + the 27+9 coef
  scalars loaded once per program.
- **Local accumulation + single atomic flush** of `grad_router[3,H]`,
  `grad_norm[H]`, `grad_pcw[9,3]`, `grad_ccw[3,3]` per program (~16× fewer atomics).
- `num_warps=8`; all math fp32, `bf16` I/O; exp-based `_tanh`; exact `1/2304`.
- **No list comprehensions / list literals of Triton values** (the build rejects
  them) — everything unrolled into named scalars.

## Why the search is complete (genuine convergence)
The algorithm is the intended memory-bound optimum (fused, read-once,
row-blocked). All three tuning dimensions were swept and every neighbor of c012
regresses:
- `BLOCK_ROWS`: {8:7.55, 16:8.08, 32:7.47}@nw16 ; {8:10.55, **16:12.02**}@nw8
- `num_warps`: {16:8.08, **8:12.02**, 4:7.19}@BR16
- loop pipelining (`tl.range num_stages=2`): 11.65 (< 12.02)

The two most recent perf candidates (c014 BR=8, c015 pipelining) both regressed.
The only remaining idea — exact H-tiling to remove the ~44% masked lanes — is
high-risk/low-value: masked loads are predicated off and do not touch the memory
bottleneck; the multi-pass tiled alternative was already measured slower
(c006 tiled 4.3× < c007 single-pass 4.6×); and a single-pass exact-2304 width is
impossible because `tl.arange` requires a power-of-two length. No justified next
candidate remains.

